data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"### Introduction\n\nThe French Revolution, spanning from 1789 to 1799","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", remains one of the most transformative events in modern world history. It was a period of radical social and political upheaval that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" shattered the centuries-old absolute monarchy of France, dismantled the feudal system, and introduced pioneering concepts of human rights, democracy","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", and citizenship. The revolution did not occur in a vacuum; it was the explosive culmination of deep-seated systemic crises. Understanding its causes and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" consequences reveals how a nation’s struggle for liberty reshaped the political landscape of the entire Western world.\n\n---\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"### Causes of the French Revolution\n\nThe causes of the French Revolution were complex, arising from a combination of social, financial","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", intellectual, and political factors.\n\n#### 1. Social Inequality: The Three Estates\nThe primary catalyst of the revolution was the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" rigid and deeply unequal social structure of the *Ancien Régime*. French society was divided into three rigid classes, known","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" as Estates:\n*   **The First Estate** (the clergy) and **the Second Estate** (the nobility) enjoyed immense wealth,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" political power, and exemption from most taxes, despite making up only about two percent of the population.\n*   **","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"The Third Estate** comprised the remaining 98 percent of the population, including wealthy merchants (the bourgeoisie), urban laborers,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" and impoverished peasants. This vast majority bore the entire tax burden of the state while possessing virtually no political representation or social mobility","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":".\n\n#### 2. Financial Crisis and Famine\nThis social inequality was exacerbated by a catastrophic financial crisis. By the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" late 1780s, France was on the brink of bankruptcy. Decades of costly foreign wars, including","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the Seven Years' War and France's substantial financial support for the American Revolution, had drained the royal treasury. This","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" was compounded by the extravagant spending of King Louis XVI and his court at Versailles. \n\nTo make matters worse, a series of","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" severe droughts and harsh winters in the late 1780s led to widespread crop failures. The price of bread","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"—the staple food of the French working class—skyrocketed, causing mass starvation, inflation, and widespread bread riots.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\n#### 3. The Influence of the Enlightenment\nIntellectually, the French public was increasingly influenced by the Enlightenment","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". Philosophers like Voltaire, Jean-Jacques Rousseau, and Montesquieu challenged the divine right of kings, advocating instead for","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" individual liberty, religious tolerance, and a government based on a social contract. These ideas circulated widely in salons and pamphlets,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" providing the intellectual framework and vocabulary for reform.\n\n#### 4. Political Incompetence\nPolitically, King Louis XVI proved","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" to be an indecisive and weak leader. When he attempted to address the financial crisis by taxing the nobility, they","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" resisted. Desperate, the king convoked the Estates-General in 1789—a legislative assembly representing","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the three estates that had not met since 1614—inadvertently opening the floodgates of revolution.\n\n","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"---\n\n### Consequences of the French Revolution\n\nThe consequences of the French Revolution were profound, reshaping France and reverberating across","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the globe.\n\n#### 1. Abolition of the Monarchy and the Rise of the Republic\nThe immediate consequence of the revolution","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" was the collapse of the absolute monarchy. When the Third Estate broke away from the Estates-General to form the National Assembly","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", they asserted that sovereignty resided in the people, not the monarch. The storming of the Bastille on July ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"14, 1789, symbolized the popular overthrow of royal tyranny. By 1792,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the monarchy was officially abolished, and France was declared a republic. King Louis XVI and Queen Marie Antoinette were eventually executed by","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" guillotine for treason.\n\n#### 2. The Reign of Terror and Political Instability\nThe vacuum left by the monarchy led","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" to extreme political instability. External threats from coalition forces of European monarchs, combined with internal counter-revolutionary rebellions, pushed","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the revolution into its radical phase: the Reign of Terror (1793–1794). Led by","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Maximilien Robespierre and the Jacobins, the government executed tens of thousands of suspected \"enemies of the revolution.\"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This chaos eventually paved the way for the rise of Napoleon Bonaparte, a brilliant military general who seized power in a coup d'état","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" in 1799, establishing an authoritarian regime that consolidated many revolutionary reforms while ending the democratic experiment.\n\n#### 3","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":". Social and Legal Reforms\nDespite the return to authoritarianism under Napoleon, the social fabric of France was permanently altered.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Feudalism was abolished, stripping the nobility and clergy of their historic privileges. The Napoleonic Code was introduced, establishing","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the principle of legal equality for all male citizens, protecting property rights, and secularizing the state. Education was standardized, and the power","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of the Catholic Church was significantly curtailed.\n\n#### 4. The Spread of Nationalism and Liberalism\nGlobally, the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" revolution unleashed the powerful forces of nationalism and liberalism. The concept of the \"nation-state,\" where citizens owe allegiance to their","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" country rather than a monarch, spread across Europe. The revolution's ideals of \"Liberty, Equality, Fraternity\" inspired oppressed peoples worldwide,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" directly influencing the Haitian Revolution—the first successful slave revolt in the modern world—and the Latin American wars of independence.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe French Revolution was born from the volatile combination of systemic social inequality, economic ruin, Enlightenment","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" philosophy, and weak governance. While it resulted in immense bloodshed and political instability, its consequences were monumental. It dismantled the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" feudal order, redefined the relationship between citizens and the state, and disseminated democratic ideals across the globe, laying the foundational stones of modern democratic","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" societies.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

